From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: John Ericson <John.Ericson@Obsidian.Systems>
Date: Mon, 17 Aug 2026 11:29:51 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] virtiofs: a read-only Virtio FS (FUSE-over-virtqueue) client

Two new modules, because a module cannot be in both `kernel/drv` and
`kernel/fs`:

  * `drv/vtfs` (`uts/common/io/virtiofs/`) owns the Virtio FS PCI device
    (1af4:105a).  It is modelled on `vioblk`: `virtio_init()`,
    `virtio_init_features()`, one request queue, `virtio_init_complete()`,
    and a pool of request slots each holding a `virtio_chain_t` and a
    bounce buffer.  Requests are synchronous -- submit, then sleep until
    the interrupt handler returns the chain.  Devices are found by the
    `tag` in their configuration space.

  * `fs/virtiofs` (`uts/common/fs/virtiofs/`) is the file system, modelled
    on `hsfs`: read-only, with the ISO9660 parsing replaced by FUSE
    message marshalling and disk reads replaced by `FUSE_READ`.

`VOP_GETPAGE` follows the conventional `pvn_getpages()` /
`pvn_read_kluster()` / `pageio_setup()` / `bp_mapin()` shape, with the
`FUSE_READ` round trip where a disk based file system would call its
strategy routine.  It is load-bearing rather than decorative: the intended
use is mounting a host `/nix/store`, whose contents are ELF objects that
are mapped rather than `read(2)`.

FUSE opcodes implemented: `FUSE_INIT`, `FUSE_LOOKUP`, `FUSE_FORGET`,
`FUSE_GETATTR`, `FUSE_OPEN`, `FUSE_READ`, `FUSE_RELEASE`, `FUSE_OPENDIR`,
`FUSE_READDIR`, `FUSE_RELEASEDIR`, `FUSE_READLINK`, `FUSE_STATFS`.  This
is not a FUSE subsystem: there is no `/dev/fuse`, no library ABI and no
upcall path, only the wire structures a Virtio FS device speaks.

Mount with `mount -F virtiofs [-o uid=,gid=] <tag> <dir>`.

Untested at runtime.  Both modules compile and link cleanly, and neither
has an undefined symbol beyond what its `-N` declarations promise.

Assisted-by: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
